## Task

Build a complete ML.NET-based support ticket classifier in a .NET 10 Web API project at `/tmp/sv-83767db4190a4d638aa673b6b0805a9f/TicketClassifier`.

## Current State

The project has:
- `TicketClassifier.csproj` targeting `net10.0` with `AddControllers`/`MapControllers`
- `Program.cs` with minimal setup
- `Data/tickets.csv` with 6 rows: Subject, Description, Priority, Category (Billing/Technical/General)

## Requirements

### 1. Expand training data
The CSV only has 6 rows — far too few for ML. Expand `Data/tickets.csv` to ~50 rows with realistic, varied support tickets across all 3 categories (Billing, Technical, General), with Priority 1-3. Keep the same CSV format. Make the text realistic and varied.

### 2. Add NuGet packages
Add `Microsoft.ML` (version 4.*) and `Microsoft.Extensions.ML` (version 4.*) to the csproj.

### 3. Create Models
Create `Models/TicketData.cs`:
```csharp
using Microsoft.ML.Data;

namespace TicketClassifier.Models;

public class TicketData
{
    [LoadColumn(0)] public string Subject { get; set; } = "";
    [LoadColumn(1)] public string Description { get; set; } = "";
    [LoadColumn(2)] public float Priority { get; set; }
    [LoadColumn(3)] public string Category { get; set; } = "";
}
```

Create `Models/TicketPrediction.cs`:
```csharp
using Microsoft.ML.Data;

namespace TicketClassifier.Models;

public class TicketPrediction
{
    [ColumnName("PredictedLabel")] public string Category { get; set; } = "";
    public float[] Score { get; set; } = [];
}
```

Create `Models/TicketRequest.cs` (the API DTO):
```csharp
namespace TicketClassifier.Models;

public class TicketRequest
{
    public string Subject { get; set; } = "";
    public string Description { get; set; } = "";
    public int Priority { get; set; }
}
```

Create `Models/TicketPredictionResponse.cs`:
```csharp
namespace TicketClassifier.Models;

public class TicketPredictionResponse
{
    public string Category { get; set; } = "";
    public Dictionary<string, float> Probabilities { get; set; } = new();
}
```

### 4. Create ModelTrainer service
Create `Services/ModelTrainer.cs`. This service:
- Uses `MLContext(seed: 42)` for reproducibility
- Loads CSV data from `Data/tickets.csv`
- Splits into train/test (test fraction 0.2)
- Builds a pipeline:
  - Featurize Subject and Description with `FeaturizeText`
  - Concatenate text features + Priority into "Features"
  - Map Category to a key label via `MapValueToKey`
  - Use `SdcaMaximumEntropy` multiclass trainer
  - Map key back to value with `MapKeyToValue`
- Trains the model
- Evaluates on test set, logs metrics (MicroAccuracy, MacroAccuracy, LogLoss) using `ILogger`
- Saves the model to `Data/TicketModel.zip`
- Implements `IHostedService` to train on app startup

```csharp
using Microsoft.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;

namespace TicketClassifier.Services;

public class ModelTrainer : IHostedService
{
    private readonly ILogger<ModelTrainer> _logger;
    private readonly string _dataPath;
    private readonly string _modelPath;

    public ModelTrainer(ILogger<ModelTrainer> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
        _dataPath = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "Data", "tickets.csv");
        _modelPath = Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "Data", "TicketModel.zip");
    }

    public Task StartAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var mlContext = new MLContext(seed: 42);

        var data = mlContext.Data.LoadFromTextFile<TicketData>(
            _dataPath, hasHeader: true, separatorChar: ',', allowQuoting: true);

        var split = mlContext.Data.TrainTestSplit(data, testFraction: 0.2);

        var pipeline = mlContext.Transforms.Text.FeaturizeText("SubjectFeaturized", nameof(TicketData.Subject))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Text.FeaturizeText("DescriptionFeaturized", nameof(TicketData.Description)))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Concatenate("Features", "SubjectFeaturized", "DescriptionFeaturized", nameof(TicketData.Priority)))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapValueToKey("Label", nameof(TicketData.Category)))
            .Append(mlContext.MulticlassClassification.Trainers.SdcaMaximumEntropy("Label", "Features"))
            .Append(mlContext.Transforms.Conversion.MapKeyToValue("PredictedLabel"));

        _logger.LogInformation("Training ticket classification model...");
        var model = pipeline.Fit(split.TrainSet);

        var predictions = model.Transform(split.TestSet);
        var metrics = mlContext.MulticlassClassification.Evaluate(predictions);

        _logger.LogInformation("Model evaluation metrics:");
        _logger.LogInformation("  MicroAccuracy:  {MicroAccuracy:F4}", metrics.MicroAccuracy);
        _logger.LogInformation("  MacroAccuracy:  {MacroAccuracy:F4}", metrics.MacroAccuracy);
        _logger.LogInformation("  LogLoss:        {LogLoss:F4}", metrics.LogLoss);
        _logger.LogInformation("  LogLossReduction: {LogLossReduction:F4}", metrics.LogLossReduction);

        mlContext.Model.Save(model, data.Schema, _modelPath);
        _logger.LogInformation("Model saved to {ModelPath}", _modelPath);

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    public Task StopAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken) => Task.CompletedTask;
}
```

### 5. Create Prediction Controller
Create `Controllers/PredictController.cs`:
```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.Extensions.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;

namespace TicketClassifier.Controllers;

[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class PredictController : ControllerBase
{
    private readonly PredictionEnginePool<TicketData, TicketPrediction> _predictionPool;

    public PredictController(PredictionEnginePool<TicketData, TicketPrediction> predictionPool)
    {
        _predictionPool = predictionPool;
    }

    [HttpPost]
    public ActionResult<TicketPredictionResponse> Predict([FromBody] TicketRequest request)
    {
        var input = new TicketData
        {
            Subject = request.Subject,
            Description = request.Description,
            Priority = request.Priority
        };

        var prediction = _predictionPool.Predict(input);

        var labels = new[] { "Billing", "General", "Technical" };
        var probabilities = new Dictionary<string, float>();
        for (var i = 0; i < prediction.Score.Length && i < labels.Length; i++)
        {
            probabilities[labels[i]] = prediction.Score[i];
        }

        return Ok(new TicketPredictionResponse
        {
            Category = prediction.Category,
            Probabilities = probabilities
        });
    }
}
```

### 6. Update Program.cs
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.ML;
using TicketClassifier.Models;
using TicketClassifier.Services;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddControllers();
builder.Services.AddHostedService<ModelTrainer>();
builder.Services.AddPredictionEnginePool<TicketData, TicketPrediction>()
    .FromFile(Path.Combine(AppContext.BaseDirectory, "Data", "TicketModel.zip"), watchForChanges: true);
var app = builder.Build();
app.MapControllers();
app.Run();
```

### 7. Ensure the CSV is copied to output
Add to `TicketClassifier.csproj` inside the existing PropertyGroup or as a new ItemGroup:
```xml
<ItemGroup>
  <Content Include="Data\tickets.csv">
    <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
  </Content>
</ItemGroup>
```

### Implementation Steps

1. Edit `Data/tickets.csv` — expand to ~50 rows of realistic data
2. Edit `TicketClassifier.csproj` — add packages and Content item
3. Create directories: `Models/`, `Services/`, `Controllers/`
4. Create all the model/service/controller files listed above
5. Edit `Program.cs` with the updated content
6. Run `dotnet restore` then `dotnet build` to verify everything compiles
7. If build fails, fix the issues and rebuild

### IMPORTANT NOTES
- Use `PredictionEnginePool` (from `Microsoft.Extensions.ML`) — never a singleton `PredictionEngine` in ASP.NET Core
- The labels array in the controller should actually be read dynamically. A simpler approach: since we know the 3 categories, hardcode them but sort alphabetically (that's how ML.NET orders them by default for MapValueToKey). Actually, the order depends on the training data. A better approach is to just return the Score array alongside the predicted label without mapping to names, OR get the slot names from the model schema. Let me use the VBuffer approach to get actual label names from the model.

Actually, for the controller, let's get the label names from the prediction engine's output schema to be correct:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public ActionResult<TicketPredictionResponse> Predict([FromBody] TicketRequest request)
{
    var input = new TicketData
    {
        Subject = request.Subject,
        Description = request.Description,
        Priority = request.Priority
    };

    var prediction = _predictionPool.Predict(input);

    return Ok(new TicketPredictionResponse
    {
        Category = prediction.Category,
        Probabilities = GetLabelScores(prediction.Score)
    });
}

private Dictionary<string, float> GetLabelScores(float[] scores)
{
    var engine = _predictionPool.GetPredictionEngine();
    var schema = engine.OutputSchema;
    var scoreColumn = schema["Score"];
    var slotNames = new VBuffer<ReadOnlyMemory<char>>();
    scoreColumn.GetSlotNames(ref slotNames);
    var labels = slotNames.DenseValues().Select(v => v.ToString()).ToArray();

    var result = new Dictionary<string, float>();
    for (var i = 0; i < labels.Length && i < scores.Length; i++)
    {
        result[labels[i]] = scores[i];
    }
    return result;
}
```

Hmm, actually `PredictionEnginePool` doesn't expose `GetPredictionEngine()` publicly. Let's keep it simple and just return the predicted category and the raw scores. The label ordering from ML.NET's MapValueToKey is alphabetical by default. So we can safely use alphabetical ordering: Billing, General, Technical.

Use the simpler approach: hardcode the sorted labels. This is fine for a demo. In a production system you'd extract them from the model schema.

Do all of this work. Build the project to verify it compiles. Do NOT run the app (it would try to start a server). Just make sure it builds successfully.

